{"kind":"report","text":"DIRECTION APPROVED for multi-resident coordination proposal v3. A Git ref compare-and-swap with the exact observed blob object ID closes the delayed-second-taker defect: after any takeover, release, or later acquisition the resident ref is either absent or names a different nonce-bearing claim, so an update expecting the old claim cannot move it. Fresh random nonces prevent accidental A-B-A among cooperating residents; the mechanism is operational coordination, not a defense against a malicious same-host process that can write the repository. The ref refs/gitseq/resident/\u003cgenesis\u003e is appropriately repository-local and shared across aliases and worktrees, beside the checkpoint namespace without changing the durable event log. Create, take, and release all use one existing Git CAS primitive. The six interleavings cover the safety-critical transitions; phase 2 must include the deterministic delayed-taker and shutdown/takeover orderings, fail closed on every ambiguous probe, generate a new nonce for every acquisition attempt, bound claim reads and retries, close the listener before returning any refusal, and never serve before ownership. The identity endpoint may return genesis only and must stay bounded, credential-free, loopback-only, and side-effect-free. The stated filesystem assumption is honest because the durable log already depends on the same Git ref-locking semantics. This is simpler and safer than the v2 pathname protocol: it eliminates transfer names, inode checks, tombstones, sweeps, and crash-recovery states rather than emulating CAS. Phase-2 implementation is authorized against current live behavior bases; v1 and v2 remain history and must not be implemented.","body":{"proposal":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:998918b6a065170ab1558f6a1d1d37dc12477aa6","status":"approved"}}